<PageShell title="Scholarship of Teaching">
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      My strongest area of scholarly activity is the Scholarship of Teaching, particularly the application
      and ongoing evaluation of evidence-informed practices in asynchronous online Biology education. My
      course development work has involved intentionally applying principles of active learning, alignment
      of learning outcomes and assessments, adaptive learning, authentic assessment, student guidance, and
      meaningful feedback to the design of online courses. I have also used student evaluations, student
      performance, and faculty feedback to identify areas for improvement and to inform subsequent course
      revisions. This work reflects an ongoing process of applying pedagogical knowledge, evaluating its
      effectiveness, and refining instructional practice.
    </p>
    <p>
      A significant example is my redesign of BIO-125 and BIO-126. After teaching BIO-125 in its previous
      format, I identified opportunities to reduce repetitive assignments and replace activities that
      emphasized task completion with assessments that more intentionally supported learning outcomes. The
      resulting course design incorporates adaptive learning through Knewton Alta, guided study materials,
      application-based assignments, laboratory experiences, low-stakes assessments, and multiple
      instructional modalities. I have continued to evaluate these courses through IDEA feedback, student
      performance, and my own observations and am using this information to identify further revisions.
    </p>
  </div>
</PageShell>
